AB  - arwin (1859) founded his theory of evolution on the premise that natural selection promotes the maintenance and spread of traits that enhance the survival and fecundity of individuals. From the outset, however, Darwin realized that there existed a widespread and diverse set of traits that defied explanation as promoters of survival or fecundity. These traits were the gaudy ornaments and burdensome armaments seen primarily in males of many species of animals. Accounting for such traits posed one of the great challenges to the generality of Darwin's theory of evolution, and he devoted an entire book (Darwin 1871) and much of his personal correspondence (see Cronin 1991) to explaining their existence. So distinct were these secondary sexual characteristics from traits that had evolved through natural selection that, to explain them, Darwin evoked a special form of natural selection, which he termed sexual selection. Darwin (1871) proposed that instead of promoting traits that enhance survival or fecundity, sexual selection promotes the spread and maintenance of traits
